+# The first components
+
+**Status: proposed (2026-11-19), for review.** Which components CompMD should have first, so an author finds what
+they reach for: what documentation frameworks ship, which npm component packages are used most, and what CompMD covers
+already, with a ranked list.
+
+## Decisions (proposed)
+
+1. **The ranking weighs what people write, not only documentation.** CompMD's documents are posts, newsletters and
+   notes as well as docs, so the frameworks' counts (below) are one signal; a figure with a caption or a chart ranks
+   higher than a framework count gives it, and an API field table, which only API docs need, lower.
+2. **What CompMD has is not listed again.** Callouts are GitHub's alerts (spec §2.3), math is in the base syntax (the
+   sandbox draws it with Temml), columns and card grids are `row` and `grid` (spec §9), Mermaid and wireframes are packs,
+   and code blocks are highlighted by the host.
+3. **A component without code is a `compmd` definition**, styled with the visual tokens (spec §9.4), as the spec's own
+   `card` and `callout` are: no code, no sandbox, nothing to load. Code is for what a definition can't do: a component
+   that changes when the reader clicks (tabs, a collapsible), and drawing from data (charts).
+4. **A component that needs code is written in the plain binding** (spec §7.6), with no framework to load, unless the
+   package it wraps is a framework's.
+5. **Only what runs in a strict frame is in the first list:** no network, no frames, images only from `data:` and
+   `blob:` (spec §15). What needs more is listed under [Later](#later), with what it waits for.
+6. **One package per kind,** by licence (MIT, BSD, ISC or Apache-2.0), size, and whether it draws without the network
+   and prints.
+
+## The first list
+
+| # | Component | Written as | Wraps | Binding | Why |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | `tabs` (and code groups) | a leaf of its own code | — | plain | 10 of 11 frameworks ship tabs. A slot's items carry their params (spec §7.4), so each tab's label is its item's; a code group is tabs whose items are code blocks. Its fallback is every tab in order, under its label. |
+| 2 | `details` (a collapsible) | its own code | — | plain | 9 of 11 frameworks. Raw HTML's `<details>` exists, but HTML is opaque (spec §2.3): no components or Markdown structure inside it. A component draws a native `<details>` around its slot. Its fallback is its content, under its summary. |
+| 3 | `card`, `link-card` | `compmd` definitions on `box`, in a `grid` | — | none | 8 of 11 frameworks; the spec's own example of a definition. A link card is a card whose title is a link. |
+| 4 | `steps` | its own code | — | plain | 7 of 11 frameworks, and how-tos in posts. Numbers its items and draws the line between them; its fallback is the items in order. |
+| 5 | `figure` | a `compmd` definition | — | none | Few frameworks ship it, but posts and newsletters caption their images. An image, a caption below it, the caption as its text alternative too. |
+| 6 | `badge` | a `compmd` definition, inline | — | none | 6 of 11 frameworks; "new", "beta", a version. Its fallback is its text. |
+| 7 | `chart` | a leaf over data rows | `@observablehq/plot` | plain | Charts from `md.data` are what CompMD's data imports are for (spec §8.6). Plot draws SVG, which prints and follows the scheme with CSS, with no network; ISC, 127 KB gzipped. |
+| 8 | `graphviz` | a code renderer for ```` ```dot ```` | `@viz-js/viz` | plain | The other text-to-diagram language people write, as Mermaid is; MIT, WebAssembly (which the frames now allow), no network. |
+| 9 | `data-table` | a leaf over data rows | `gridjs` | plain | A table of data the reader sorts and searches. MIT, 16 KB gzipped. Its fallback is the table's caption. |
+| 10 | `music` | a code renderer for ```` ```abc ```` | `abcjs` | plain | Sheet music from text, which a plain renderer shows as its source; MIT. Drawing only: playing it needs a sound font from the network. |
+| 11 | `qr-code` | a leaf | `qrcode` | plain | A link on paper: newsletters, posters and slides, printed. MIT, 9 KB gzipped, an SVG. |
+| 12 | `timeline` | `compmd` definitions on `box` | — | none | Dates and what happened, in a post or notes; a list of items is enough, so no package. |
+
+Each is a pack of its own, or a few of them share one (`tabs`, `details`, `steps`, `card`, `figure` and `badge` as one
+pack of the basics, since documents use them together); how they are grouped is the packs' design, after this list.
+
+## What else was weighed
+
+**Charts.** Chart.js (MIT, 70 KB gzipped, the most used) draws on a canvas, which prints at the screen's resolution;
+ECharts (Apache-2.0) has the most kinds but is 368 KB gzipped; Vega-Lite (BSD-3) is a JSON spec, and would fit a code
+renderer (```` ```vega-lite ````) better than a leaf; Recharts is React's. Highcharts and amCharts are commercial, and
+ApexCharts' licence is no longer MIT.
+
+**Tables.** TanStack Table is headless (the component would draw the table itself), AG Grid is 392 KB gzipped, and
+Handsontable is commercial.
+
+**Diagrams.** D2's WebAssembly is 6 MB gzipped. tldraw's licence needs a key for production use. Excalidraw is planned
+already (CMD-18), once a component can keep a drawing (CMD-17).
+
+**Math.** KaTeX and MathJax are not needed: math is in the base syntax, and the sandbox draws it with Temml.
+
+## Later
+
+What the first list leaves out, and what it waits for:
+
+| Kind | Packages | Waits for |
+|---|---|---|
+| Image zoom, a gallery, a before-and-after slider | `medium-zoom`, `photoswipe`, `img-comparison-slider` (all MIT) | a component reading the document's images: a strict frame loads images only from `data:` and `blob:` (G1) |
+| 3D models | `@google/model-viewer` (Apache-2.0) | the same, for model files, and WebGL in the frames |
+| Video, YouTube, a post on a social network | `lite-youtube-embed`, `media-chrome` | frames inside a frame (the frames' `frame-src 'none'`) and the network: a permissive pack at least (G2) |
+| Maps | `leaflet` (BSD-2), `maplibre-gl` (BSD-3), with `pmtiles` | tiles from the network (a permissive pack), or a tile file in the pack (G3) |
+| A live code playground | `@codesandbox/sandpack-react` | a hosted bundler, in a frame |
+| An API field table | — | API docs only (decision 1); a `compmd` definition when it's asked for |
+| A tooltip, a banner, `kbd` | — | 2 to 3 frameworks each; `kbd` is in raw HTML's allowlist already |
+
+## Gaps
+
+- **G1. A component and the document's images.** A strict frame's policy allows images from `data:` and `blob:` only,
+  so a component can't show an image the document links. A zoom, a gallery or a slider needs the host to hand the
+  images in (as the slot's items, already loaded), which no design says yet.
+- **G2. Embeds.** A frame can't open a frame, and a strict one has no network, so an embed is a permissive pack's at
+  best; until then, a link card is the honest fallback.
+- **G3. Maps.** A map's tiles come from a server, which only a permissive pack reaches; a tile file in the pack (PMTiles)
+  makes a small offline map possible, its size to measure.
+- **G4. Tabs and collapsibles in the core pack.** They are what authors reach for first, and every page with one would
+  load a frame for it. Implemented by the processor in `@compmd/core`, like the input components (spec §8.7), they'd
+  cost nothing, but that is a spec change; as a pack, nothing changes. To decide before the pack is built.
+- **G5. The numbers.** Weekly downloads count every install, including those pulled in by other packages (cytoscape and
+  elkjs come with Mermaid), so they say what is used, not what authors chose; no study of what docs authors use most
+  was found.
+
+## The research
+
+Read on 2026-11-19. Downloads are npm's for the week of 12 to 18 November 2026
+(`api.npmjs.org/downloads/point/last-week/<name>`), licences from the registry, sizes from Bundlephobia (minified and
+gzipped, the package's entry point).
